KingMod source security review - 2026-10-15

Scope: static inspection of startup, process access, network requests, file writes,
command execution, persistence indicators, embedded binaries, and build configuration.
The game/mod/injector was not executed. This is not a guarantee that all code is safe.

Findings:
- No clear RAT command channel, browser credential theft, startup persistence,
  scheduled tasks, or antivirus-tampering code was identified in reviewed custom code.
- Removed the HTTP helper fallback that disabled TLS peer and hostname verification.
- Optional Discord webhooks transmit match data, player names and ranks to a
  user-configured endpoint. Default: disabled and endpoint empty.
- Optional LAN discovery broadcasts a player name/custom tag and accepts presence
  packets. Default: disabled. LAN hosting exposes a small status/password service.
- PsyNet dumping writes raw game request/response bodies locally when explicitly
  enabled. Default: disabled. These logs may contain sensitive account/session data.
- Map downloads/extraction trust external services and archive parsers. The legacy
  map downloader invokes PowerShell Expand-Archive; the other path may launch an
  installed 7-Zip/WinRAR tool. These are map features, not evidence of a RAT.
- The separate KernelInjector reads/writes another process through a kernel-driver
  interface and creates a remote thread. It is separate from the KingMod DLL build;
  this behavior is powerful and was not executed or certified safe.
- Discord RPC extracts its embedded DLL to a process-specific temporary path,
  loads it for local Discord IPC and removes it during shutdown. Both bundled
  discord-rpc.dll files have valid local Authenticode signatures by Discord Inc.
- The bundled FreeType static library has not been independently verified against
  a trusted reproducible upstream build. Signatures and imports are limited evidence.

Antivirus: Microsoft Defender custom scan could not run: 0x800106ba, RPC server
unavailable. There is no successful antivirus scan or runtime sandbox result.

Build fix: RocketSim is now a native Visual Studio project dependency. A standard
Release|x64 build compiles its source and links the produced library automatically.

